lesson physics-puzzles-6 v=1 format=puzzles grade=6 unit=9 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Physics puzzles: sun, sound, energy and the moon"
  goal "Use the sixth year's rules, for the tilted Earth, heat, sound, forces, energy, levers and the moon, to solve puzzles with no method given."

  puzzle stars=1 {
    show physics.tilt-noon lat=70 s=0 easy-lat=80 hard-lat=50 hard-s=1
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show physics.wave-hertz w=5 easy-w=3 hard-w=7
  }
  puzzle stars=1 {
    show physics.sledge-net v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  level easy {
    puzzle stars=1 {
      show physics.store-falling-water m=5 h=3
    }
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show physics.heat-rod-fair k=2 easy-k=3 hard-k=0
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show physics.store-more-out o=1100 easy-o=1200 hard-o=1500
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show physics.eclipse-not-monthly o=1 easy-o=-1
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show physics.moment-tips a=6 p=2 b=5 q=3 easy-a=8 easy-p=4 easy-b=3 easy-q=1 hard-a=4 hard-p=3 hard-b=3 hard-q=4
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show physics.wave-length-for v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show physics.gen-water-for-lamps v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  level medium hard {
    puzzle stars=3 {
      show physics.moondrop-shelf te=0.6 hard-te=0.8
    }
  }
  level hard {
    puzzle stars=3 {
      show physics.hut-latitude lat=85
    }
  }

  grown-ups """
    Let the child try each puzzle alone first, on the moon or at the table; the hints and answers
    are on this sheet, not on theirs. Every puzzle comes from a lesson this year and can be started
    by looking closely at its drawing: where the sun is overhead, how many waves cross the screen,
    which arrows are longer, how far each weight is from the pivot. The one-star puzzles use a rule
    once. The two-star puzzles each need a reason: what else changed in a test, why a machine cannot
    give out more energy than it takes in, why there is not an eclipse every month, and which way a
    lever turns without working out every number. The three-star puzzles are for a quiet moment: a
    length found from a note, water worked back from lamps, a fall timed on the moon from one on
    Earth. Ask for the reason as well as the answer. A puzzle left unsolved today is often solved
    another day.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the sun is in June at 80 degrees north, the screens and sledges carry the smallest numbers, a fourth one-star puzzle works out the joules from falling water, and the three-star moon puzzle is left for another day."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the noon sun is worked out in December, the lever's two moments are close, the machine's claim is furthest out, and a fourth three-star puzzle finds the hut's latitude from the sun's height at noon and at midnight."
  }
}
